# 6. CORE USER EXPERIENCE

## Public reader experience

A visitor should be able to:

1. arrive at the home page
2. understand what the publication is about immediately
3. scan featured and recent stories
4. filter by category/tag
5. open an article
6. read comfortably on mobile
7. see correctly optimized Cloudinary media
8. discover related stories
9. open an author page
10. search
11. copy/share the article URL
12. navigate back naturally

## CMS author experience

An authenticated author should be able to:

1. open Studio
2. create a post
3. enter title/dek/excerpt
4. create rich content
5. upload images through Cloudinary
6. insert images into content
7. assign categories
8. assign tags
9. select cover image
10. edit SEO metadata
11. preview
12. save as draft
13. publish
14. see publication state
15. revise content
16. inspect revision history
17. manage media
18. manage profile

## Admin experience

Where the current project supports administrative ownership/roles, allow authorized users to:

- moderate comments
- manage categories
- manage tags
- manage publication settings
- manage authors
- manage SEO defaults
- review audit events

Do not invent a giant RBAC framework for v1.

Start with a small, explicit role model such as:

- owner
- editor
- author
- moderator

Implement only the permissions actually used by the product.

