- tag
- article body
- image figure
- video figure
- callout
- related stories
- article share controls

## Studio

- studio sidebar
- studio header
- status badge
- post table
- filter bar
- editor shell
- editor toolbar
- media picker
- upload dropzone
- media grid
- media detail panel
- revision list
- category manager
- tag manager
- settings form
- publishing sidebar

---

# 29. DASHBOARD VISUAL DESIGN

The studio should NOT look like a standard admin template.

Instead:

```text
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  [GEOMETRIC MARK] CINDERPRESS               + New     Avatar │
├───────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│               │                                              │
│ OVERVIEW      │  Good morning                               │
│               │  Your publication at a glance                │
│ Posts         │                                              │
│ Drafts        │  ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐     │
│ Published     │  │ Drafts   │ │ Published│ │ Views    │     │
│ Media         │  └──────────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────┘     │
│ Categories    │                                              │
│ Tags          │  Recent stories                              │
│ Comments      │  ─────────────────────────────────────────   │
│ Authors       │  Story title                         Draft   │
│ Revisions     │  Story title                      Published  │
│               │                                              │
│ SETTINGS      │                                              │
│ Publication   │                                              │
│ Profile       │                                              │
│ SEO           │                                              │
│               │                                              │
└───────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Use restrained cards.

Do not create a wall of rounded cards.

---

# 30. BRAND / LOGO IMPLEMENTATION

Create the logo as an original geometric mark.

Possible construction:

```text
        /──────/
       /      /|
      /──────/ |
      |      | /
      |      |/
      └──────┘
```

But do not literally use this primitive cube as the finished mark.

The final mark should communicate an editorial page being folded, layered, indexed, or published.

Use a custom vector path or a small set of geometric primitives.

Create:

- favicon
- app icon
- horizontal lockup
- icon-only mark
- monochrome variant

The mark should still be recognizable at 16–24px.

---

# 31. THEME DETAILS

## Light mode

Primary canvas:

warm white/paper

Main text:

near-black

Secondary surfaces:

very light warm gray

Borders:

neutral warm gray

Accent:

orange

## Dark mode

Optional but strongly recommended.

Use:

- charcoal/ink background
- warm off-white text
- dark graphite surfaces
- subtle gray borders
- orange accent

Do not invert everything to a harsh black-white contrast.

Do not use purple/blue gradients in either theme.

---

# 32. MICROINTERACTIONS

Good examples:

- logo subtly rotates one geometric layer on first load
- upload zone highlights border on drag-over
- publish button transitions from idle → publishing → published
- autosave status transitions smoothly
- cards have restrained hover elevation
- search suggestions appear with a short opacity/translate transition
- mobile drawer uses a clean slide
- article images reveal softly as loaded

Bad examples:

- cards constantly floating
- glowing buttons
- animated gradients
- rotating 3D logos continuously
- excessive parallax
- animated background particles

---

# 33. EMPTY STATES

Every meaningful empty state should have a purpose-built message and optional geometric/isometric illustration.

Examples:

No posts:

"Your publication is still a blank page."

Action:

"Create your first story"

No media:

"Your media library is ready for its first asset."

Action:

"Upload media"

No comments:

"No conversations yet."

No search results:

"No stories matched that search."

Avoid generic sad-face illustrations.

---

# 34. ERROR STATES

Errors must feel polished and useful.

Examples:

Upload failed:

"That upload didn't make it to the library."

With:

"Try again"

and technical details behind a disclosure where appropriate.

Do not show raw stack traces.

---

# 35. SETTINGS

Publication settings:

- publication name
- description
- logo
- favicon
- default metadata
- social links
- locale
- timezone
- default category behavior
- comment policy

Profile:

- display name
- avatar
- bio
- website
- social links

SEO:

- site title
- default description
- default social image
- robots behavior
- canonical base

---

# 36. COMMENTS

Build a simple moderation workflow.

Public:

- submit comment
- visible submission state
- no raw moderation controls

Studio:

- pending
- approved
- rejected
- spam

Moderators/editors can update status if authorized.

Do not build nested threaded discussions in v1 unless the repo already contains that requirement.

---

# 37. ANALYTICS ABSTRACTION

Build a tiny internal event abstraction, for example conceptually:

```ts
trackEvent({
  name: "post_view",
  postId,
  metadata
})
```

Do not immediately add a complex analytics platform.

Track only useful events:

- post_view
- search
- media_upload
- post_created
- post_updated
- post_published
- comment_submitted
- comment_moderated

Keep the implementation replaceable.

---

# 38. TEST STRATEGY

At minimum, test:

## Unit

- slug generation
- content validation
- permission checks
- reading-time calculation
- metadata generation
- Cloudinary transformation helpers
- publishing validation

## Integration

- authenticated request handling
- post CRUD
- media metadata creation
- publishing
- comment moderation
- category/tag assignment

## E2E

Test the actual browser flows:

1. public homepage loads
2. article opens
3. search works
4. sign-in opens
5. authenticated user can access Studio
6. unauthorized user cannot access Studio
7. create post
8. save draft
9. upload Cloudinary media
10. insert image into article
11. preview
12. publish
13. public article appears
14. edit published article
15. cache/revalidation works
16. sign out
17. signed-out access is blocked
18. mobile layout remains usable

Use the installed browser verification skill / agent-browser capability after major milestones.

---

# 39. VISUAL QA

Do not assume the UI is correct because TypeScript compiles.

Use browser verification and inspect:

- mobile 360px
- mobile 390px
- tablet
- desktop
- large desktop

Look specifically for:

- horizontal overflow
- clipped text
- broken editor layouts
- modal overflow
- bad image crops
- excessive shadows
- inconsistent radii
- purple/blue leakage
- generic template appearance
- weak typography
- accidental gradients
- poor focus states
- layout shift
- slow initial render

If you see a generic AI-generated look, redesign it.

---

# 40. PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT ARCHITECTURE

Target:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    USER[Browser] --> CF[Cloudflare]
    CF --> NEXT[Next.js App on Cloudflare Runtime]

    NEXT --> AUTH[InsForge Auth]
    NEXT --> D1[(Cloudflare D1)]
    NEXT --> CLD[Cloudinary]

    CLD --> CDN[Cloudinary CDN]

    CF --> WAF[Cloudflare Security Layer]
    NEXT --> OBS[Cloudflare Logs / Observability]
```

The exact current Cloudflare Next.js deployment mechanism must be verified through the connected Cloudflare documentation/MCP before final deployment.

Do not use a deprecated Cloudflare Pages pattern merely because an old tutorial says to.

---

# 41. ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE POLICY

Create a clean `.env.example`.

Possible categories:

```text
# App
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_PUBLICATION_NAME=

# InsForge
NEXT_PUBLIC_INSFORGE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_INSFORGE_ANON_KEY=
# plus any server-side secret required by the current official InsForge integration

# Cloudinary
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_UPLOAD_PRESET=
# server-only API key/secret as required by the current secure integration

# Cloudflare
# bindings/configuration should use the platform's official mechanism,
# not arbitrary values invented by the agent
```

Do not finalize exact secret names until current SDK/docs are checked.

Never expose a server-only secret through `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`.

---

# 42. DEVELOPMENT / PRODUCTION SEPARATION

Development can have:

- seed data
- local fixtures
- controlled preview content

But:

```text
DEVELOPMENT MOCKS
      !=
PRODUCTION AUTH
      !=
PRODUCTION DATA
```

Any development bypass must require an explicit development-only condition.

Example policy:

```text
if development AND explicit local mock flag:
    allow fixture behavior
else:
    require real authentication
```

Do not make "NODE_ENV !== production" alone the only guard for sensitive bypasses.

Never ship a fake admin session.

---

# 43. MIGRATIONS

Use real database migrations.

Do not modify the production database schema by silently running ad-hoc SQL from application startup.

Migration files must be:

- deterministic
- ordered
- reviewable
- repeatable in clean environments

Seed files should be separate from migration files.

---

# 44. CONTENT SEED

Create realistic sample content for local development.

Include:

- 1 publication
- 3–5 categories
- 8–12 stories
- 3 authors
- tags
- realistic excerpts
- realistic cover image references where safe
- drafts
- published content
- comments in moderation states

Do not use lorem ipsum.

The sample content should look like an actual technology/editorial publication.

Possible editorial themes:

- technology
- web infrastructure
- design
- developer tools
- digital culture
- product engineering

---

# 45. WRITING STYLE

Public editorial copy should be:

- confident
- concise
- intelligent
- human
- not corporate filler
- not AI-generated sounding
- not stuffed with buzzwords

Studio UI copy should be:

- concise
- informative
- calm
- actionable

Avoid:

"Supercharge your workflow with next-generation AI-powered content orchestration."

Prefer:

"Create, edit, and publish stories from one workspace."

---

# 46. HOME PAGE DEMO CONTENT STYLE

The design should visually support strong editorial headlines such as:

"Why the fastest software is often the least complicated."

"Inside the new generation of edge-native applications."

"The quiet return of industrial interface design."

These are placeholders for demo content only.

Do not write dozens of fake startup slogans.

---

# 47. DESIGN SYSTEM TOKENS

Define central tokens for:

- spacing
- typography
- radii
- borders
- shadows
- colors
- motion
- container widths
- editorial reading width

Avoid hard-coded visual values repeated throughout components.

Conceptually:

```text
spacing
├── xs
├── sm
├── md
├── lg
├── xl
└── 2xl

radius
├── sm
├── md
└── lg

shadow
├── subtle
├── medium
└── floating

container
├── page
├── content
└── reading
```

---

# 48. NO DESIGN DEBT

Before declaring UI complete:

- inspect every page at mobile size
- inspect every page at desktop size
- compare spacing rhythm
- verify typography
- verify button hierarchy
- verify forms
- verify loading states
- verify empty states
- verify error states
- verify dark mode if included
- remove inconsistent one-off styles

Do not ship five different button styles.

Do not ship five different card radii.

Do not ship arbitrary colors.

---

# 49. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES

## PHASE 1 — REPOSITORY + SERVICE AUDIT

First:

- inspect repo
- inspect package manager
- inspect existing Next.js/React version
- inspect current routes
- inspect existing database
- inspect Cloudflare configuration
- inspect Cloudinary configuration
- inspect InsForge connection
- inspect available MCP servers
- inspect design/UI skills

Write a short internal implementation map.

Do not delete functioning work without reason.

## PHASE 2 — FOUNDATION

Implement:

- design tokens
- typography
- global CSS
- theme
- layout system
- logo
- navigation
- base UI
- app shell

Validate responsive behavior early.

## PHASE 3 — AUTH

Implement real InsForge auth.

Validate:

- sign-up
- sign-in
- session
- sign-out
- protected Studio
- unauthorized state

Do not proceed with fake production auth.

## PHASE 4 — DATABASE

Implement:

- D1 migrations
- schema
- indexes
- relationships
- seed data
- safe query layer
- authorization helpers

## PHASE 5 — PUBLIC SITE

Implement:

- homepage
- story listing
- article page
- categories
- tags
- author pages
- search
- archive

## PHASE 6 — STUDIO

Implement:

- dashboard
- posts list
- post creation
- editor
- save draft
- publish
- revision history
- categories/tags
- media library
- settings

## PHASE 7 — CLOUDINARY MEDIA

Implement:

- secure upload authorization
- media library
- image delivery
- responsive transformations
- article insertion
- cover image selection
- alt text/caption/credit

## PHASE 8 — SEO + DISCOVERABILITY

Implement:

- metadata
- canonical
- OG
- JSON-LD
- sitemap
- robots
- RSS

## PHASE 9 — SECURITY + PERFORMANCE

Audit:

- auth
- authorization
- secret exposure
- XSS
- input validation
- upload security
- caching
- bundle size
- client component usage

## PHASE 10 — BROWSER VERIFICATION

Run full end-to-end browser checks.

Fix everything found.

## PHASE 11 — DEPLOYMENT

Use current official Cloudflare deployment guidance.

Verify:

- build
- deployment
- environment variables
- D1 binding
- auth
- Cloudinary
- public pages
- Studio
- logs

## PHASE 12 — FINAL QA

Do not stop at "build succeeded."

The definition of completion is functional + visual + secure + responsive + deployable.

---

# 50. AGENT DECISION RULES

When several technical choices are possible:

Choose the simplest option that:

1. is production-safe
2. works on Cloudflare
3. works with the current Next.js runtime
4. keeps Cloudinary focused on media
5. keeps InsForge focused on authentication
6. keeps D1 focused on relational application data
7. minimizes dependencies
8. performs well on mobile
9. can be understood by another agent later

Do not optimize for novelty.

Optimize for reliability.

---

# 51. COMMON FAILURE MODES TO AVOID

## Failure: localStorage-first architecture

Do not do this.

Wrong:

```ts
const userId = localStorage.getItem("userId") || crypto.randomUUID()
```

Correct:

```text
real authenticated session
        ↓
server verification
        ↓
authorized identity
        ↓
D1 query
```

## Failure: InsForge used as a fake wrapper

Do not add InsForge just to display a login screen.

Authentication must actually protect private data and routes.

## Failure: Cloudinary as the database

Do not store the entire CMS inside asset metadata.

Use D1.

## Failure: duplicate storage

Do not store every Cloudinary image again in R2 or D1.

Store references/metadata.

## Failure: client-side authorization

Do not trust hidden buttons.

Server-side authorization is mandatory.

## Failure: generic UI generator aesthetic

Delete and redesign anything that becomes:

- purple
- neon
- gradient-heavy
- glass-heavy
- dashboard-template-like
- visually noisy

## Failure: desktop-first publishing

Do not sacrifice the reader experience on Android/mobile.

## Failure: editor shipped without autosave/error handling

Content creation needs durable saves and clear state.

## Failure: no revision strategy

Every publish/update operation should have a predictable revision model.

---

# 52. DEFINITION OF DONE

The project is complete only when all of the following are true:

### Architecture

- Cloudflare is the application/edge platform.
- D1 is the structured application database.
- Cloudinary is the media platform.
- InsForge is real authentication.
- Responsibilities are clearly separated.

### Authentication

- real sign-up
- real sign-in
- real session persistence
- real sign-out
- protected Studio
- server-side authorization
- no identity localStorage
- no fallback admin
- no dev token in production

### Publishing

- create
- edit
- draft
- preview
- publish
- revise
- categorize
- tag
- author
- cover image

### Media

- upload
- store Cloudinary reference
- optimized delivery
- responsive images
- alt text
- captions
- media library

### Public web

- homepage
- stories
- article
- category
- tag
- author
- search
- archive
- RSS
- sitemap
- robots

### Visual

- orange/warm-white industrial editorial theme
- geometric/isometric original mark
- strong typography
- no neon gradients
- no purple/blue SaaS aesthetic
- no generic lock/shield visual language
- no excessive glassmorphism
- responsive design
- restrained motion

### Quality

- type-safe
- linted
- tested
- browser-verified
- mobile-verified
- accessibility reviewed
- production build passes
- deployment verified

